\title{The Metric Growth Spectrum of the Berggren Tree of Pythagorean Triples}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{10 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
The Berggren tree organises the primitive Pythagorean triples into an infinite ternary
tree, rooted at $(3,4,5)$, in which each node has exactly three children given by three
fixed unimodular matrices and each primitive triple occurs exactly once. Working in the
Euclid seed coordinates $(m,n)$, $m>n>0$, and embedding each node in the hyperbolic upper
half-plane at $z(m,n)=(n+i)/m$, we determine the metric growth of the tree completely.

We prove a \emph{window lemma}: $\log m\le d(i,z(m,n))\le\log m+\log 2$, so hyperbolic
displacement equals the logarithm of the larger seed coordinate to within an additive
$\log2$. We introduce the \emph{silver potential} $\Phi(m,n)=m+(\sqrt2-1)n$, show that it
is multiplied by exactly $\lam=1+\sqrt2$ by the middle Berggren move and strictly
contracted, with explicit slack, by the other two, and deduce that the maximum of $\Phi$
over depth $k$ is exactly $\lam^{k+1}$, attained by the pure-middle (Pell) spine and by no
other node; indeed every other depth-$k$ node satisfies $\Phi\le\lam^{k+1}-\sqrt2$. The
Pell spine dominates every depth-$k$ node coordinatewise, hence simultaneously and
uniquely maximises seed, hypotenuse and potential, and Binet formulas give the sharp
constant $\max_{\text{depth }k}m/\lam^{k+1}\to(2+\sqrt2)/4=0.853553\ldots$

Consequently the metric growth exponent of the tree is exactly
$\log(1+\sqrt2)=0.881373\ldots$, strictly between $\log2$ and $\log3$. We then study the
spectrum of realised rates $\lim_k d(i,z_k)/k$ along infinite paths. We refute the natural
conjecture that the rate is a function of the asymptotic frequency of the expanding move:
the two periodic paths $(B_2B_3)^\infty$ and $(B_2B_2B_3B_3)^\infty$, neither of which
uses $B_1$, both have middle-move frequency $1/2$ but have the distinct exact rates
$\tfrac12\log(2+\sqrt5)$ and $\tfrac12\log(2+\sqrt3)$. Positively, we exhibit a
two-parameter family of periodic paths $(B_2^aB_3^b)^\infty$, $a$ odd, of exact rate
$\log\sigma(a,b)/(a+b)$ with $\sigma(a,b)=(T+\sqrt{T^2+4})/2$ and
$T(a,b)=P_{a+1}+2bP_a+P_{a-1}$ a Pell trace, and prove these rates dense in
$[0,\log(1+\sqrt2)]$. Combined with the universal ceiling, the closure of the metric
growth spectrum is exactly $[0,\log(1+\sqrt2)]$, realised entirely without $B_1$. Finally
we translate the geometry into arithmetic: a node of hypotenuse $\ge N$ has depth
$\ge(\log N-\log2)/(2\log(1+\sqrt2))-1$, the Pell spine achieves depth
$\le(\log N+\log2)/(2\log(1+\sqrt2))$, and two nodes sharing a hypotenuse $N$ have depth
sum at least $(\log N-\log 2)/\log(1+\sqrt2)-2$.
\end{abstract}

